.TH gen_bit 3 "24 Mar 1995" "CADC"
.SH NAME
gen_bit, bitand, bitcmp, bitcomp, bitor, bitprint, bitscan, bitshift, bitxor \- Perform bit operations on byte arrays.
.SH SYNOPSIS
.LP
.nf
.ft B
#include "gen_types.h"
#include "gen_bit.h"
.ft
.fi
.LP
.TP 7n
byte
.RB * bitand ( 
byte
.RI * array1 ,
byte
.RI * array2 ,
byte
.RI * result ,
int
.I length
)
.TP 7n
int 
.BR bitcmp ( 
byte
.RI * array1 ,
byte
.RI * array2 ,
int
.I length
)
.TP 7n
byte
.RB * bitcomp ( 
byte
.RI * array ,
byte
.RI * result ,
int
.I length
)
.TP 7n
byte
.RB * bitor ( 
byte
.RI * array1 ,
byte
.RI * array2 ,
byte
.RI * result ,
int
.I length
)
.TP 7n
int
.BR bitprint ( 
char
.RI * string ,
byte
.RI * array ,
int
.I length
)
.TP 7n
byte
.RB * bitscan ( 
char
.RI * string ,
byte
.RI * result ,
int
.I length
)
.TP 7n
byte
.RB * bitshift ( 
byte
.RI * array ,
byte
.RI * result ,
int
.IR length ,
int
.I shift
)
.TP 7n
byte
.RB * bitxor ( 
byte
.RI * array1 ,
byte
.RI * array2 ,
byte
.RI * result ,
int
.I length
)
.Es
.SH DESCRIPTION
These routines do various manipulations on bit strings stored by
byte arrays.  All of these functions allow the length of the bit
strings to be specified in bits.  If any partial bytes are specified,
the high order bits of the byte will be used.
.LP
.B bitand(\|)
does a logical and of the bits in byte 
.I array1 
and byte 
.I array2
and puts the result into
.IR result .
The number of bits in the arrays is specified by 
.IR length .
The return value is result.
.LP
.B bitcmp(\|)
compares the bits in 
.I array1 
to byte 
.I array2
and returns 0 if the arrays are equal, a value < 0 if 
.I array1
< 
.IB array2 ,
or a value > 0 if 
.I array1
> 
.IB array2 .
The number of bits in the arrays is specified by 
.IR length .
.LP
.B bitcomp(\|)
does a ones complement of the bits in
.I array1 
and puts the result into
.IR result .
The number of bits in the array is specified by 
.IR length .
The return value is result.
.LP
.B bitor(\|)
does a logical or of the bits in byte 
.I array1 
and byte 
.I array2
and puts the result into
.IR result .
The number of bits in the arrays is specified by 
.IR length .
The return value is result.
.LP
.B bitprint(\|)
formats the byte array in
.I array
into character string
.IR string .
The number of bits in the arrays is specified by 
.IR length .
The size of 
.I string 
must be at least 
.RI ( length
* 2) + 3.
.LP
.B bitscan(\|)
Scans a string formatted like "0x0123..." into a bit array.
.I string 
is the input string, 
.I result 
is the output bit string, and 
.I length
is the length of the bit string.  Any bytes not set in the
input string will be set to zero.
The return value is result.
.LP
.B bitshift(\|)
Does a shift of the bit array.  This function is not implemented.
.LP
.B bitxor(\|)
does a exclusive or of the bits in byte 
.I array1 
and byte 
.I array2
and puts the result into
.IR result .
The number of bits in the arrays is specified by 
.IR length .
The return value is result.
.SH NOTES
.LP
These functions use the same error checking as the string routines,
i.e. if anything goes wrong a segmentation fault will be the result.

.SH BUGS
.LP
None known.

